1
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.733
Welcome to Event-Driven

2
00:00:03.733 --> 00:00:07.800
Agentic Document Workflows
built in partnership with LlamaIndex.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.000
I'm delighted that

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.333
the instructor for this course

5
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:14.400
is Laurie Voss, whose VP of developer relations at LlamaIndex.

6
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.833
Thanks, Andrew.

7
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:18.633
I'm jazzed to be back here with you
and teach this course.

8
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:19.900
Agentic document workflows are

9
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.000
are agent-based applications
that you can use

10
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.200
to automate an end-to-end document
processing workflow.

11
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.833
While RAG systems answers
simple questions about your data,

12
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:34.000
agentic workflows can be built on
top of RAG to help you process

13
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.733
input documents in more sophisticated ways.
In the architecture you learn about,

14
00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:42.266
an agent will identify key information
that you will need to carry the tasks

15
00:00:42.666 --> 00:00:45.900
and then retrieve relevant materials
using RAG.

16
00:00:46.166 --> 00:00:50.466
And finally, combine collected information
into a structured output.

17
00:00:51.066 --> 00:00:51.966
For example,

18
00:00:51.966 --> 00:00:55.866
say you want to review a contract
for compliance with certain regulations.

19
00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:59.033
The agent could parse the contracts,
extract the key

20
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:02.833
clauses, and match them with relevant
clauses from a knowledge base

21
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.666
of regulatory requirements,
and finally generate a compliance summary.

22
00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:09.933
As another example, say you want to enrich

23
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:12.966
a set of invoices
with standardized product information.

24
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.033
The agent can extract the item
descriptions from the invoice,

25
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.600
match them with the closest product code
from the catalog using RAG,

26
00:01:20.866 --> 00:01:24.566
and it append the standardized information
to that invoice.

27
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:28.966
In this course, you apply this type
of workflow to a practical application

28
00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:33.100
in which you build an agent that use the
resume to follow the job application form.

29
00:01:34.066 --> 00:01:34.966
Laurie will walk you through

30
00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:38.200
how to build this from scratch
using LlamaIndex's

31
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.200
workflow abstraction, which is a good way
to build event-driven systems,

32
00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:47.866
which is a key design pattern for building
efficient sets of agents.

33
00:01:48.666 --> 00:01:50.566
That's right. LlamaIndex's

34
00:01:50.566 --> 00:01:54.466
workflows is an event-driven architecture
that you will use to create your agent.

35
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.566
You will encapsulate the agent's logic
in a chain of steps, where each

36
00:01:58.566 --> 00:02:01.566
step emits events to trigger
further steps.

37
00:02:01.666 --> 00:02:04.966
You will learn how you can use code
branching and looping within the workflow.

38
00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:08.800
Create concurrent events, and collect
multiple events at a given step.

39
00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:12.566
You'll apply these concepts to build
your form filling agent step by step.

40
00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:16.966
You'll start by setting up your agent's
RAG capability to parse the given resume,

41
00:02:17.266 --> 00:02:20.266
load it into a vector store,
and create a query engine.

42
00:02:20.566 --> 00:02:24.300
Then you'll get your agent to parse
the job application form, convert the

43
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.366
blank spaces into a series of questions,
and send them to the RAG pipeline.

44
00:02:28.866 --> 00:02:29.566
You'll then provide

45
00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:33.166
feedback to your agent and iterate
together on the returned answers.

46
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.566
You will communicate your feedback
through text and then using your voice.

47
00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:40.066
Many people have worked
to create this course.

48
00:02:40.066 --> 00:02:43.466
I'd like to thank Logan Markewich
from LlamaIndex,

49
00:02:43.466 --> 00:02:46.866
who helped a great deal,
and from DeepLearning.AI, Hawraa Salami

50
00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:49.366
also contributed to this course.

51
00:02:49.366 --> 00:02:51.666
Event-driven
workflows are really important

52
00:02:51.666 --> 00:02:54.100
design pattern,
and I see more and more businesses

53
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.966
using it to design large language
model-powered applications.

54
00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:00.966
I think you enjoy learning
about these concepts.

55
00:03:00.966 --> 00:03:03.666
So, please go on to the next
video and let's get started.

